<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-methionine biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: MET| (Met) is one of the proteinogenic amino acids. In addition to its role in protein biosynthesis, L-methionine is required for a number of important cellular functions, including the initiation of protein synthesis, the methylation of DNA, rRNA and xenobiotics, and the biosynthesis of cysteine, phospholipids and polyamines. In all metzoa |FRAME: MET| is an essential amino acid (an indispensable amino acid that cannot be synthesized &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt;) and must be supplied in the diet. Some bacteria can synthesize methionine using organic sulfur through transsulfuration of |FRAME: O-SUCCINYL-L-HOMOSERINE| with |FRAME: CYS| to form |FRAME: L-CYSTATHIONINE|. Cystathionine is then cleaved to |FRAME: HOMO-CYS|, which is methylated to methionine (|FRAME: HOMOSER-METSYN-PWY|) |CITS: [3309561]|. Plants use a modification of this pathway, where |FRAME: O-PHOSPHO-L-HOMOSERINE|, instead of |FRAME: O-SUCCINYL-L-HOMOSERINE|, is the intermediate converting homoserine to cystathionine (this pathway). Other bacteria, as well as yeast and fungi, can use a different route in which they directly assimilate inorganic sulfur by a sulfhydrylation (see |FRAME:HSERMETANA-PWY|). In that route |FRAME: HS|, the final product of microbial sulfate reduction (see MetaCyc pathway |FRAME: SO4ASSIM-PWY|) reacts with &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-acetylated homoserine, with replacement of the acetyl group by sulfide to form |FRAME: HOMO-CYS|, which is once again methylated to methionine (see |FRAME: PWY-5344|). Most methanogenic archaea utilize yet a different pathway. While these organisms also use |FRAME: HS| as the sulfur source, the other precursor for |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| production is |FRAME: L-ASPARTATE-SEMIALDEHYDE|, produced from |FRAME: L-ASPARTATE| (see |FRAME: PWY-7977|). Methylation of |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| is catalyzed by either a cobalamin-dependent enzyme (|FRAME: EC-2.1.1.13 &quot;EC 2.1.1.13&quot;|) or a cobalamin-independent enzyme (|FRAME: EC-2.1.1.14 &quot;EC 2.1.1.14&quot;|), depending upon the species or growth conditions |CITS: [9409150][12948640]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; In plants the nitrogen and carbon skeleton of methionine comes from |FRAME: HOMO-SER|, the sulfur atom from |FRAME: CYS|, and the methyl group from folate. Methionine biosynthesis in plants differs from that in microorganisms, as |FRAME: O-PHOSPHO-L-HOMOSERINE|, rather than |FRAME: O-SUCCINYL-L-HOMOSERINE|, is the intermediate converting |FRAME: HOMO-SER| to |FRAME: L-CYSTATHIONINE|. Plants also have additional enzymes (|FRAME: EC-2.1.1.10|) that can methylate |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| to |FRAME: MET|, using |FRAME: CPD-397| and |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| as the methyl donors. However, those enzymes participate in the |FRAME: PWY-5441| rather than in &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; methionine biosynthesis |CITS: [11309147]|.</Description>
